<section id="path-a-book" data-path="A" aria-labelledby="book-heading"><h2 class="sec" id="book-heading" data-cp="head">Path A · Explore spinning objects</h2>
<p>Work through the shared opening, then choose the exploration that fits your goal. Record your ideas before opening each explanation, summarize what you learn from the videos, and use the interactive model to plan a test. Your responses and the original predictions saved when you open an explanation travel with your final answers in <b>Copy my answers</b>.</p>
<p>Bring a question from any step to your AI Dojo. Describe what you noticed and what you think is happening; ask the Dojo to help you investigate the part you cannot yet explain.</p>
<p id="book-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
<h3 data-cp="head">1 · Start with something that puzzles you</h3>
<p>Have you skated, watched Olympic skating, played with a top, or seen someone holding a spinning wheel? Pick something you remember or wonder about. If these are new to you, imagine holding one end of a spinning wheel’s axle and letting go of the other end.</p>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_notice"><b>My starting observation and prediction</b></label><p id="st-bk_notice">Describe the situation you want to explore. What do you expect to happen, and what makes you think so? Include a question you want to investigate.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_notice" data-key="bk_notice" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_notice"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_notice" aria-controls="reveal-bk_notice" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my starting idea and watch the gyroscope video</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_notice">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_notice" class="book-answer" hidden>
<div class="book-video"><iframe loading="lazy" title="Principles of gyroscopes · Prof. Sathya" data-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/kbg7UE3qwqs?start=140&end=430" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p><a href="https://youtu.be/kbg7UE3qwqs?t=140" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Principles of gyroscopes · Prof. Sathya in a new tab</a></p>
<p>In this clip, “no external force” should be read as “no net outside torque,” meaning no net outside twisting effect. A supported spinning wheel can turn its axis around under gravity’s torque; a freely oriented symmetric gyroscope can keep its axis pointing in the same direction when the net outside torque is negligible.</p>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_gyro_summary"><b>What I learned from the gyroscope video</b></label><p id="st-bk_gyro_summary">Describe one observation or explanation from the video in your own words. How does it connect to your starting idea, and what remains unclear?</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_gyro_summary" data-key="bk_gyro_summary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_gyro_summary"></textarea></div>
<h3 data-cp="head">2 · Describe a spin with quantities</h3>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_units"><b>My prediction about spin measurements</b></label><p id="st-bk_units">Two dots sit at different distances from the center of one turning wheel. In one full turn, which dot travels farther? Which completes more turns? Explain your prediction.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_units" data-key="bk_units" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_units"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_units" aria-controls="reveal-bk_units" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my prediction and explore spin measurements</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_units">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_units" class="book-answer" hidden>
<div class="book-video"><iframe loading="lazy" title="Ladybug on a turntable · Prof. Sathya" data-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/2-032DBce-o?start=0" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p><a href="https://youtu.be/2-032DBce-o?t=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Ladybug on a turntable · Prof. Sathya in a new tab</a></p>
<p>Both dots complete one turn, but the outer dot travels a longer circle. Spin rate describes turns per second; angular speed describes radians per second. One full turn is 2π radians, so multiply turns per second by 2π to get rad/s.</p>
<details><summary>Try a radian with a jar and string</summary><p>Cut a string as long as the jar’s radius, from its center to its edge. Lay that length along the rim. The angle it spans at the center is one radian. A little more than six such lengths fit around the rim. Repeat with a larger jar and explain why one turn still takes about 6.28 radii.</p></details>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_units_summary"><b>What I learned about measuring spin</b></label><p id="st-bk_units_summary">Explain the difference between a dot’s speed along the rim and the wheel’s spin rate. Convert 30 turns per minute to rad/s, showing how the units change. Identify any point you still need help with.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_units_summary" data-key="bk_units_summary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_units_summary"></textarea></div>
<h3 data-cp="head">3 · Explore where the mass is</h3>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_inertia"><b>My prediction about mass and distance</b></label><p id="st-bk_inertia">Compare a small 1 kg mass held 2 m from an axis with a small 4 kg mass held 1 m from it. Which arrangement would be harder to speed up around the axis? Explain your reasoning before watching.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_inertia" data-key="bk_inertia" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_inertia"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_inertia" aria-controls="reveal-bk_inertia" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my reasoning and explore moment of inertia</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_inertia">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_inertia" class="book-answer" hidden>
<div class="book-video"><iframe loading="lazy" title="Linear vs angular motion · Prof. Sathya" data-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/mVFrEitAnRs?start=154&end=550" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p><a href="https://youtu.be/mVFrEitAnRs?t=154" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Linear vs angular motion · Prof. Sathya in a new tab</a></p>
<p>Moment of inertia describes how mass is distributed around a chosen axis. For a small mass at distance r, I = mr². The two arrangements each have I = 4 kg·m², so they respond equally to the same torque. For several masses, add their contributions; for an extended object, use a relationship appropriate to its shape and axis.</p>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_inertia_summary"><b>What I learned about mass and distance</b></label><p id="st-bk_inertia_summary">Use a detail from the video to explain why distance from the axis matters. Compare the explanation with your prediction, and describe how mass distribution could matter in your chosen situation.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_inertia_summary" data-key="bk_inertia_summary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_inertia_summary"></textarea></div>
<h3 data-cp="head">4 · Choose a relationship to investigate</h3>
<p>Choose the exploration closest to your goal. You can return and try the other one; each keeps its responses. Your submission includes the selected exploration and any responses you recorded while trying the other one.</p>
<fieldset class="pick choice" data-cp="radio" data-name="book_focus" data-cp-label="My Path A exploration"><legend>My exploration</legend><div class="radios col"><label><input type="radio" name="book_focus" value="speed"> How spin speed changes when mass moves</label><label><input type="radio" name="book_focus" value="direction"> How a spinning axis changes direction</label></div></fieldset>
<div data-book-branch="speed" hidden>
<h3 data-cp="head">My exploration · Changing spin speed</h3>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_idea"><b>My prediction before the spin-speed explanation</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_idea">A small mass turns on a light rod. It moves to half its original distance from the axis while no net outside torque acts. How do you expect its spin rate to change, and why?</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_idea" data-key="bk_speed_idea" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_idea"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_speed_idea" aria-controls="reveal-bk_speed_idea" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my prediction and explore the relationship</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_speed_idea">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_speed_idea" class="book-answer" hidden>
<div class="book-video"><iframe loading="lazy" title="Conservation of angular momentum · Prof. Sathya" data-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/orAtL67eKc8?start=119&end=458" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p><a href="https://youtu.be/orAtL67eKc8?t=119" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Conservation of angular momentum · Prof. Sathya in a new tab</a></p>
<p>For rotation about a fixed axis, angular momentum is L = Iω. When the same system experiences negligible net outside torque, I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. Halving the small mass’s distance makes its I one quarter as large, so its spin rate becomes four times as large. A chair and a person contribute to the total I too; they cannot be left out of that calculation.</p>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_summary"><b>What I learned from the spin-speed video</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_summary">Explain the relationship in your own words using a detail from the video. Identify the system and axis, and explain when you would expect this relationship to apply.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_summary" data-key="bk_speed_summary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_summary"></textarea></div>
<p>In this model, two small equal masses move inward or outward on a rotating platform. B is the fixed moment of inertia of the platform and everything that stays fixed to it. Each mass is m. The total I is B + 2mr²; the model assumes negligible net outside torque and keeps the same masses in the system.</p>
<div class="book-model" data-model="speed"><h4>Try a model of moving masses</h4>
<label class="book-number">Fixed moment of inertia B (kg·m²)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="b" data-key="model_speed_b" aria-label="Fixed moment of inertia B (kg·m²)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Each moving mass m (kg)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="mass" data-key="model_speed_mass" aria-label="Each moving mass m (kg)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Initial distance r₁ (m)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="r1" data-key="model_speed_r1" aria-label="Initial distance r₁ (m)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Final distance r₂ (m)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="r2" data-key="model_speed_r2" aria-label="Final distance r₂ (m)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Initial spin rate ω₁ (rad/s)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="omega" data-key="model_speed_omega" aria-label="Initial spin rate ω₁ (rad/s)"></label>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_predict"><b>My model prediction · spin speed</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_predict">Enter your chosen model inputs above. Predict the final spin rate with its unit, show your calculation, and explain the condition you are assuming. Write a new prediction before each run.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_predict" data-key="bk_speed_predict" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_predict"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn" data-run-model="speed">Save my prediction and run</button><p data-model-status="speed" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><svg viewBox="0 0 360 180" class="book-sim" role="img" aria-label="View from above of two masses on a spinning platform"><circle cx="180" cy="90" r="75" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><g data-spinner="speed"><line x1="105" y1="90" x2="255" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="105" cy="90" r="9" fill="#117a75"/><circle cx="255" cy="90" r="9" fill="#117a75"/></g></svg>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_trials"><b>My saved model runs · spin speed</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_trials">Each run records your inputs, prediction and model output here.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_trials" data-key="bk_speed_trials" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_trials" readonly></textarea></div>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_compare"><b>What I make of the model runs</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_compare">Compare the model output with your prediction. Explain what the comparison supports and what it cannot establish about a real skater or chair.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_compare" data-key="bk_speed_compare" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_compare"></textarea></div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_speed_boundary"><b>My prediction about the condition</b></label><p id="st-bk_speed_boundary">A spinning person drags a foot on the floor while pulling their arms inward. Could the same constant-angular-momentum calculation predict their final speed? Explain your reasoning.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_speed_boundary" data-key="bk_speed_boundary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_speed_boundary"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_speed_boundary" aria-controls="reveal-bk_speed_boundary" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my reasoning and check the condition</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_speed_boundary">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_speed_boundary" class="book-answer" hidden>
<p>The floor exerts an outside torque, so the person’s angular momentum changes. You need information about that torque and how long it acts to predict the final speed. In your own check, identify possible outside torques and whether they are small enough for your approximation.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div data-book-branch="direction" hidden>
<h3 data-cp="head">My exploration · A spinning axis changes direction</h3>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_idea"><b>My prediction before the direction explanation</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_idea">Imagine the same tilted spinning top turning about its own axis twice as fast. Would you expect its axis to sweep around the vertical faster, slower or at the same rate? Explain your reasoning.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_idea" data-key="bk_direction_idea" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_idea"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_direction_idea" aria-controls="reveal-bk_direction_idea" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my prediction and explore the relationship</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_direction_idea">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_direction_idea" class="book-answer" hidden>
<p>Angular momentum has a direction. A net outside torque changes angular momentum; for a rapidly spinning symmetric top, gravity can turn its direction so that the axis sweeps around the vertical. This motion is called precession. Negligible net outside torque instead preserves the angular momentum direction.</p>
<p>For slow, steady precession, Ω = Mgd / (Iω). M is the total mass (kg), d is the distance from pivot to center of mass along the axis (m), g = 9.8 m/s².</p><p>I is moment of inertia about the spin axis (kg·m²), and ω is spin rate (rad/s). Ω is precession rate (rad/s). The approximation assumes a fixed pivot, little friction, and spin much faster than precession. With the other quantities fixed, doubling ω halves Ω.</p>
<p>Read the explanation and diagrams in <a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-4-precession-of-a-gyroscope" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenStax, Precession of a Gyroscope</a>. Use them to revisit the wheel or top in the opening video. This model addresses steady precession; predicting when a slowing top falls requires more detail.</p>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_summary"><b>What I learned about a spinning axis</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_summary">Use a detail from the opening gyroscope video and the reading to explain the difference between keeping an axis direction and precessing. Explain how the condition in the relationship fits your goal, and what remains uncertain.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_summary" data-key="bk_direction_summary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_summary"></textarea></div>
<div class="book-model" data-model="direction"><h4>Try a model of steady precession</h4>
<label class="book-number">Total mass M (kg)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="mass" data-key="model_direction_mass" aria-label="Total mass M (kg)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Pivot-to-center distance d (m)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="distance" data-key="model_direction_distance" aria-label="Pivot-to-center distance d (m)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Spin-axis moment of inertia I (kg·m²)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="inertia" data-key="model_direction_inertia" aria-label="Spin-axis moment of inertia I (kg·m²)"></label>
<label class="book-number">Spin rate ω (rad/s)<input type="number" min="0.001" step="any" inputmode="decimal" data-model-param="omega" data-key="model_direction_omega" aria-label="Spin rate ω (rad/s)"></label>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_predict"><b>My model prediction · precession</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_predict">Enter your chosen model inputs above. Predict the precession rate with its unit, show your calculation, and explain the condition you are assuming. Write a new prediction before each run.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_predict" data-key="bk_direction_predict" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_predict"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn" data-run-model="direction">Save my prediction and run</button><p data-model-status="direction" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><svg viewBox="0 0 360 180" class="book-sim" role="img" aria-label="View from above of the projection of the spinning axis precessing around a fixed pivot"><circle cx="180" cy="90" r="65" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="4 5"/><g data-spinner="direction"><line x1="180" y1="90" x2="245" y2="90" stroke="#117a75" stroke-width="5"/><circle cx="245" cy="90" r="7" fill="#117a75"/></g><circle cx="180" cy="90" r="4" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_trials"><b>My saved model runs · precession</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_trials">Each run records your inputs, prediction and model output here.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_trials" data-key="bk_direction_trials" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_trials" readonly></textarea></div>
</div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_compare"><b>What I make of the model runs</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_compare">Compare the model output with your prediction. Explain what the comparison supports and what it cannot establish about a real spinning wheel or top.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_compare" data-key="bk_direction_compare" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_compare"></textarea></div>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_direction_boundary"><b>My prediction about the condition</b></label><p id="st-bk_direction_boundary">As a top slows down and begins to wobble strongly, would you keep using the steady-precession relationship? Explain which assumption you would check.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_direction_boundary" data-key="bk_direction_boundary" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_direction_boundary"></textarea></div>
<button type="button" class="btn book-reveal" data-reveal="bk_direction_boundary" aria-controls="reveal-bk_direction_boundary" aria-expanded="false" disabled>Save my reasoning and check the condition</button><p class="book-gate-note" id="gate-note-bk_direction_boundary">Write your prediction and reasoning above to open this step.</p><div id="reveal-bk_direction_boundary" class="book-answer" hidden>
<p>The approximation assumes spin dominates the motion and precession stays slow and steady. Strong wobbling or spin no longer much faster than precession calls those assumptions into question. Describe what you would observe or measure before trusting the prediction.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 data-cp="head">5 · Bring the exploration back to your goal</h3>
<div class="rbox"><label class="qt" for="bx-bk_synthesis"><b>What I now understand and want to test</b></label><p id="st-bk_synthesis">Return to your starting question. Explain what the activities helped you understand, which observation supports that understanding, and what remains uncertain. Identify the relationship and test you will carry into Tasks 1–4 below.</p><textarea class="box small" id="bx-bk_synthesis" data-key="bk_synthesis" data-cp="box" aria-describedby="st-bk_synthesis"></textarea></div>
<p>Your exploration record preserves what you tried and how your ideas developed. Your final synthesis should bring that evidence together: explain the relationship coherently, justify its assumptions and interpret your own check. Refer to useful observations from the book rather than copying every earlier response.</p><p class="body">Use Tasks 1–4 below for your final synthesis and your own check. Choose a new case, record a numerical prediction before running it, then compare. The interactive models above calculate the same relationships you are learning: agreement checks your calculation within that model, and is not independent evidence that the model describes a real object. Explain this limit if you use a model run as your check.</p>
<details><summary>Plan a measurement with a spinning chair</summary><p>For a spin-speed investigation, use a stable freely turning chair, two equal water bottles, a tape measure, a phone with <a href="https://phyphox.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">phyphox</a> and a friend to spot you. Keep turns gentle, feet ready, and stop if dizzy. Secure the phone flat on the seat or lap and use its gyroscope’s vertical-axis reading. Measure each bottle’s distance from the chair axis before and after moving it. </p><p>Include the person and chair in your estimate of total moment of inertia and explain that estimate. Predict a ratio of final to initial spin rate first, then record both rates and compare their magnitudes. Choose an accessible simulation check if this setup is unavailable.</p></details>
</section>
