<section class="excard" id="ex-b" aria-labelledby="ex-b-h">
<h3 id="ex-b-h">Example B · Path B, the playground swing</h3>
<dl>
<dt>Where I found it:</dt>
<dd>OpenStax College Physics, section 16.4, The Simple Pendulum.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>The time for one full swing grows with the square root of the length: <code>T = 2&pi; &radic;(L &divide; g)</code>, where <code>T</code> is the period in seconds (s), <code>L</code> the length in meters (m) and <code>g</code> = 9.8 m/s&sup2;.</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>The swing moves through a small angle, less than about 15 degrees from straight down, and air drag is small.</dd>
<dt>My check · Before:</dt>
<dd>A phone on a 1.0 m string should take 2.0 s per swing.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I did:</dt>
<dd>Timed ten swings with a stopwatch, starting about 10 degrees out.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I got:</dt>
<dd>20.3 s for ten swings, so 2.03 s per swing.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I make of it:</dt>
<dd>about 1% above the prediction. The length to the middle of the phone was hard to measure, and about 2 cm more length would account for the difference.</dd>
</dl>
</section>
<section class="excard" id="ex-c" aria-labelledby="ex-c-h">
<h3 id="ex-c-h">Example C · Path C, a circuit in Tinkercad Circuits</h3>
<dl>
<dt>Where I found it:</dt>
<dd>My Tinkercad Circuits build: a 9 V battery, a resistor and a red LED in one loop. The relationship is Ohm's law applied to the resistor, from OpenStax College Physics, section 20.2, Ohm's Law.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>The current is the battery's voltage minus the LED's voltage, divided by the resistance: <code>I = (V_battery &minus; V_LED) &divide; R</code>, where <code>I</code> is the current in amperes (A, shown in milliamperes, mA), <code>V_battery</code> and <code>V_LED</code> are in volts (V) and <code>R</code> is in ohms (&Omega;). This predicts the current; the LED gets brighter as the current rises, but brightness is not what the formula gives.</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>The supply stays steady, and a constant 2.0 V forward drop is a reasonable approximation for this particular LED near the intended operating current. The resistor is in series with the LED; 2.0 V is an assumed operating value, not a universal turn-on threshold.</dd>
<dt>My check · Before:</dt>
<dd>With the 470 &Omega; resistor swapped for 1,000 &Omega;, I predict (9 V &minus; 2 V) &divide; 1,000 &Omega; = 0.0070 A = 7.0 mA.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I did:</dt>
<dd>Changed the resistor in Tinkercad, added a multimeter in the loop set to current, and started the simulation.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I got:</dt>
<dd>6.9 mA.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I make of it:</dt>
<dd>about 1% below my prediction. My check compares my hand calculation with the simulator's own model of the LED, whose voltage is a little more than the 2 V I assumed, which leaves slightly less voltage across the resistor.</dd>
</dl>
</section>
